- Same group ID: consumers share the work; a partition is assigned to only one consumer in that group at a time.
- More consumers than partitions: some consumers remain idle.
- Different group IDs: each group independently receives the topic's events, subject to its own offsets and assignments.
Kafka Streams applications also use Kafka consumer groups internally, but their processing includes stream topologies and may involve state stores and repartitioning.
6. Can I use both together?
Yes. In production systems, this is common.
For example, in a payroll platform:
1. A payroll service publishes PayrollCalculated events.
2. A Kafka Streams application aggregates payroll totals by department and time window.
3. A standard Kafka consumer listens for PayrollCalculated and initiates a notification or downstream workflow.
Use separate consumer groups when both applications need to receive every event independently.
Interview-ready answer
“I use a standard Kafka consumer when I need to consume events and execute business operations,
 such as database persistence, notifications, or calling downstream services.
 I use Kafka Streams when I need continuous stream processing, such as filtering,
 transformation, aggregation, joins, or time-window calculations.
 For example, in a payroll system, a consumer can initiate employee payroll processing,
 while Kafka Streams can calculate real-time payroll totals by department.
 The choice depends on whether the primary requirement is event handling or stream processing.”

One advanced point: Kafka Streams is not automatically better for every high-throughput use case.
 Consider processing complexity, state management, ordering, delivery guarantees, event-time requirements,
  and operational overhead before choosing it.


